text:
The Story of Rust: History and Philosophy
Rust began in 2006 as a personal project by Graydon Hoare, a language engineer at Mozilla. At the time, Mozilla was grappling with the massive complexity and vulnerability surface of the Gecko rendering engine in Firefox, written in C++. In multi-threaded C++, memory corruption vulnerabilities (such as use-after-free and buffer overflows) and concurrency bugs (data races) were extraordinarily difficult to prevent, even for seasoned systems developers.
Mozilla sponsored Rust in 2009 with a distinct mission: create a modern systems language that guarantees memory safety and thread safety without sacrificing bare-metal speed or requiring a garbage collector.

Core Philosophy
The design of Rust rests on three non-negotiable pillars: 1. Safety: Eliminate undefined behavior, dangling pointers, null dereferences, and data races at compile time. 2. Speed: Provide zero-cost abstractions—constructs that compile down to assembly indistinguishable from hand-written C. 3. Concurrency: “Fearless concurrency”—the compiler prevents data races across threads using the exact same ownership model used for memory safety.
// A safe, deterministic greeting
fn main() {
    let project = "Rust";
    let year = 2026;
    println!("Welcome to {}, built for safety and speed in {}.", project, year);
}

Variables, Mutability, and Shadowing
By default, variables in Rust are immutable. Once bound to a value, the binding cannot be modified.

Mutability with mut
To allow mutation, explicitly declare the binding with mut:
fn main() {
    let mut counter = 10;
    println!("Initial counter: {}", counter);
    counter += 5;
    println!("Updated counter: {}", counter);
}


Variable Shadowing
Rust allows redeclaring a variable with the same name in the same scope using let. This is shadowing:
fn main() {
    let spaces = "   ";
    let spaces = spaces.len(); // Shadows spaces: changes type from &str to usize
    println!("Number of spaces: {}", spaces);
}
Shadowing creates a new binding, allowing type transformation without requiring distinct variable names.

Primitive Data Types in Detail
Rust is statically and strongly typed with full type inference.

Scalar Types

Integers:

Signed: i8, i16, i32, i64, i128, isize (pointer width)
Unsigned: u8, u16, u32, u64, u128, usize (pointer width, indexing)

Floating-point: f32, f64 (IEEE 754-2008)
Boolean: bool (true or false, 1 byte representation)
Character: char (4-byte Unicode scalar value)

fn main() {
    let count: usize = 42;
    let temperature: f64 = 98.6;
    let is_active: bool = true;
    let emoji: char = '🦀';

    println!("Scalar values: {}, {}, {}, {}", count, temperature, is_active, emoji);
}

Compound Types: Tuples and Arrays
Compound types group multiple values into a single type.

Tuples
A tuple has a fixed length and can contain elements of different types:
fn main() {
    let point: (i32, f64, &str) = (10, 3.14, "origin");
    let (x, y, label) = point; // Destructuring
    println!("Point {}: x = {}, y = {}", label, x, y);
    println!("Direct access: first = {}", point.0);
}


Arrays
An array has a fixed length, is stack-allocated, and elements must be of the same type:
fn main() {
    let buffer: [u8; 4] = [0xDE, 0xAD, 0xBE, 0xEF];
    println!("Array length: {}, first: 0x{:X}", buffer.len(), buffer[0]);

    let initialized = [0u32; 16]; // 16 elements initialized to 0
    println!("Initialized array length: {}", initialized.len());
}

Functions, Statements, and Expressions
Rust is an expression-oriented language. Almost every construct evaluates to a value.

Statements vs. Expressions

Statements: Instructions that perform an action and do not return a value. Ending an expression with a semicolon ; turns it into a statement returning unit ().
Expressions: Evaluate to a resulting value. Omission of the final semicolon returns the expression value.

fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -&gt; i32 {
    a + b // No semicolon: implicit return
}

fn compute_block(factor: i32) -&gt; i32 {
    let result = {
        let base = 100;
        base * factor // Expression evaluated and bound to result
    };
    result
}

fn main() {
    println!("Add: {}", add(10, 20));
    println!("Compute block: {}", compute_block(3));
}

Control Flow: if, loop, while, and for
Control flow constructs in Rust are expressions that can return values.

if as an Expression
fn main() {
    let condition = true;
    let number = if condition { 5 } else { 6 };
    println!("Number: {}", number);
}


Loops: loop, while, and for
fn main() {
    // 1. loop with value return
    let mut counter = 0;
    let result = loop {
        counter += 1;
        if counter == 10 {
            break counter * 2;
        }
    };
    println!("Loop result: {}", result);

    // 2. while loop
    let mut n = 3;
    while n &gt; 0 {
        println!("{}!", n);
        n -= 1;
    }

    // 3. for loop over iterators
    for item in 0..3 {
        println!("Index: {}", item);
    }
}

Worked example
// nonzero_demo.rs
use std::mem::size_of;
use std::num::NonZeroU32;

fn main() {
    println!("size u32={} Option&lt;u32&gt;={}", size_of::&lt;u32&gt;(), size_of::&lt;Option&lt;u32&gt;&gt;());
    println!(
        "size NonZeroU32={} Option&lt;NonZeroU32&gt;={}",
        size_of::&lt;NonZeroU32&gt;(),
        size_of::&lt;Option&lt;NonZeroU32&gt;&gt;()
    );
    match NonZeroU32::new(0) {
        None =&gt; println!("zero rejected"),
        Some(_) =&gt; unreachable!(),
    }
    let n = NonZeroU32::new(7).unwrap();
    println!("get={}", n.get());
}
get=7
zero rejected
div=42

What is Ownership and Why Does It Exist?
Ownership is Rust’s central memory management innovation. It governs how heap memory is allocated, accessed, and reclaimed without a garbage collector or manual free() calls.

The Three Rules of Ownership

Each value in Rust has an owner.
There can only be one owner at a time.
When the owner goes out of scope, the value is dropped.

fn main() {
    {
        let s = String::from("desk"); // s is valid from this point forward
        println!("Inside scope: {}", s);
    } // s goes out of scope; String memory is automatically freed here
}

Move Semantics Explained
When a heap-backed value is assigned to another binding, passed to a function, or returned from a function, ownership is moved.
fn main() {
    let s1 = String::from("orders");
    let s2 = s1; // s1 is MOVED to s2

    // println!("{}", s1); // COMPILE ERROR: value borrowed here after move
    println!("s2 owns the heap buffer: {}", s2);
}

Why Rust Moves Instead of Shallow Copying
If s1 and s2 both owned the same heap pointer, when both went out of scope, they would attempt to free the same memory twice—a catastrophic double free bug. Rust prevents this by invalidating s1 at compile time with zero runtime cost.

Copy Types and Clone

The Copy Trait
Types that live entirely on the stack and have fixed sizes implement the Copy trait. Assignment of Copy types performs a bitwise copy rather than a move:
fn main() {
    let x: i32 = 42;
    let y = x; // Bitwise copy
    println!("x = {}, y = {}", x, y); // Both valid!
}


The Clone Trait
For heap-allocated types where an explicit, potentially expensive deep copy is desired, use .clone():
fn main() {
    let s1 = String::from("records");
    let s2 = s1.clone(); // Deep allocation on heap
    println!("s1 = {}, s2 = {}", s1, s2); // Both valid
}

The Drop Trait and Resource Cleanup
Rust implements RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) via the Drop trait.

Custom Drop Implementations
struct ResourceHandle {
    name: String,
}

impl Drop for ResourceHandle {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        println!("Cleaning up resource: {}", self.name);
    }
}

fn main() {
    let _r = ResourceHandle { name: String::from("NetworkSocket") };
    println!("Resource active");
    // _r drops automatically when main exits
}
Resources (file descriptors, sockets, database handles) cannot leak due to early returns or panics because drop runs deterministically on scope exit.

Shared and Mutable References
Instead of transferring ownership, a function can access data via references. This mechanism is called borrowing.

Shared References (&T)
Shared references allow read-only access to data without taking ownership:
fn calculate_length(s: &String) -&gt; usize {
    s.len()
}

fn main() {
    let s = String::from("desk");
    let len = calculate_length(&s); // Pass reference
    println!("The length of '{}' is {}.", s, len); // s is still valid!
}


Mutable References (&mut T)
Mutable references permit modifying the borrowed value:
fn append_status(s: &mut String) {
    s.push_str(" [ACTIVE]");
}

fn main() {
    let mut task = String::from("Process orders");
    append_status(&mut task);
    println!("{}", task);
}

Dangling References Prevented at Compile Time
In languages with manual memory management, creating a pointer to memory that has been deallocated results in a dangling reference. Rust guarantees this never occurs:
// Attempting to return a reference to a local variable:
// fn dangle() -&gt; &String {
//     let s = String::from("temporary");
//     &s // ERROR: `s` does not live long enough
// }

// The idiomatic solution: transfer ownership of the value itself
fn no_dangle() -&gt; String {
    let s = String::from("valid data");
    s
}

fn main() {
    let result = no_dangle();
    println!("Received: {}", result);
}

String Slices and &str
A string slice is a reference to a contiguous sequence of UTF-8 encoded bytes in a String.
fn main() {
    let s = String::from("Hello Rust");

    let first_word = &s[0..5];
    let second_word = &s[6..10];

    println!("First: {}, Second: {}", first_word, second_word);
}

String vs &str

String: Owned, heap-allocated, resizable buffer.
&str: An immutable view into string data located on the heap, stack, or embedded in the read-only data segment of the binary (string literals).

Slice Types and Range Syntax
Slices generalize beyond strings to any contiguous sequence, such as arrays and vectors.
fn main() {
    let numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];

    let slice: &[i32] = &numbers[1..4]; // Elements 20, 30, 40
    println!("Slice: {:?}", slice);

    // Range variations:
    let start_to_two = &numbers[..2];
    let three_to_end = &numbers[3..];
    let all = &numbers[..];

    println!("Start: {:?}, End: {:?}, All: {:?}", start_to_two, three_to_end, all);
}

Defining and Using Structs
Structs allow creating custom types grouping related fields.
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Ticket {
    id: u64,
    title: String,
    resolved: bool,
}

impl Ticket {
    fn new(id: u64, title: String) -&gt; Self {
        Self { id, title, resolved: false }
    }

    fn resolve(&mut self) {
        self.resolved = true;
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut ticket = Ticket::new(101, String::from("Fix database migration"));
    println!("Ticket: {:?}", ticket);
    ticket.resolve();
    println!("Resolved: {:?}", ticket);
}

Enumerations Beyond Simple Tags
Rust enums are algebraic data types (tagged unions). Each variant can hold different types and amounts of data:
#[derive(Debug)]
enum WebEvent {
    PageLoad,
    KeyPress(char),
    Click { x: i64, y: i64 },
    Submit(String),
}

fn main() {
    let click = WebEvent::Click { x: 120, y: 340 };
    let key = WebEvent::KeyPress('q');
    println!("Events: {:?}, {:?}", click, key);
}

The match Expression in Depth
match enables exhaustive pattern matching. Every possible case must be handled.
#[derive(Debug)]
enum Priority {
    Low,
    Medium,
    High,
    Critical(u8),
}

fn inspect_priority(p: Priority) {
    match p {
        Priority::Low =&gt; println!("Low priority - queue normally"),
        Priority::Medium =&gt; println!("Medium priority - assign SLA"),
        Priority::High =&gt; println!("High priority - alert on-call"),
        Priority::Critical(level) =&gt; println!("CRITICAL (level {}) - page team immediately", level),
    }
}

fn main() {
    inspect_priority(Priority::High);
    inspect_priority(Priority::Critical(1));
}

if let and while let for Partial Matching
When you only care about matching one pattern and ignoring all others, if let and while let avoid boilerplate:
fn main() {
    let config_val: Option&lt;u32&gt; = Some(8080);

    // if let syntax
    if let Some(port) = config_val {
        println!("Binding server to port {}", port);
    }

    // while let syntax
    let mut stack = vec![1, 2, 3];
    while let Some(top) = stack.pop() {
        println!("Popped: {}", top);
    }
}

Worked example
Verified with rustc 1.99.1, edition 2024, on x86-64 Linux.
// cfg_select! picks the first arm whose predicate holds; `_` is the fallback.
cfg_select! {
    unix =&gt; {
        fn platform() -&gt; &'static str { "unix" }
    }
    windows =&gt; {
        fn platform() -&gt; &'static str { "windows" }
    }
    _ =&gt; {
        fn platform() -&gt; &'static str { "other" }
    }
}

// It also works in expression position.
fn word_size() -&gt; u32 {
    cfg_select! {
        target_pointer_width = "64" =&gt; 64,
        target_pointer_width = "32" =&gt; 32,
        _ =&gt; 0,
    }
}

#[derive(Debug)]
enum Cmd {
    Set(String),
    Del(String),
}

fn parse_port(s: &str) -&gt; Option&lt;u16&gt; {
    s.parse().ok()
}

// if-let guards: bind inside the guard, and fall through to later arms if it fails.
fn describe(cmd: &Cmd) -&gt; String {
    match cmd {
        Cmd::Set(v) if let Some(port) = parse_port(v) && port &gt;= 1024 =&gt; {
            format!("set unprivileged port {port}")
        }
        Cmd::Set(v) if let Some(port) = parse_port(v) =&gt; format!("set privileged port {port}"),
        Cmd::Set(v) =&gt; format!("set non-numeric value {v:?}"),
        Cmd::Del(k) =&gt; format!("delete {k}"),
    }
}

// The same logic before Rust 1.95: the binding has to move into the arm body,
// and the fall-through has to be duplicated by hand.
fn describe_old(cmd: &Cmd) -&gt; String {
    match cmd {
        Cmd::Set(v) =&gt; match parse_port(v) {
            Some(port) if port &gt;= 1024 =&gt; format!("set unprivileged port {port}"),
            Some(port) =&gt; format!("set privileged port {port}"),
            None =&gt; format!("set non-numeric value {v:?}"),
        },
        Cmd::Del(k) =&gt; format!("delete {k}"),
    }
}

fn main() {
    println!("platform={} word_size={}", platform(), word_size());
    for c in [
        Cmd::Set("8080".into()),
        Cmd::Set("443".into()),
        Cmd::Set("abc".into()),
        Cmd::Del("x".into()),
    ] {
        assert_eq!(describe(&c), describe_old(&c));
        println!("{c:?} -&gt; {}", describe(&c));
    }
}
cargo run -q
platform=unix word_size=64
Set("8080") -&gt; set unprivileged port 8080
Set("443") -&gt; set privileged port 443
Set("abc") -&gt; set non-numeric value "abc"
Del("x") -&gt; delete x
The assert_eq! in main checks that the guarded version and the pre-1.95 nested version agree on every input. The difference is shape. In describe_old, everything about Set has to live inside one arm, so other variants can’t be interleaved by priority. In describe, each arm states exactly what it needs.
cfg_select! appears twice: once at item level, choosing which fn platform to define, and once in expression position, as the tail of word_size. Expression-position arms are separated by commas and take a plain expression. Item-level arms take a braced block of items.

The traps
Both mistakes below are in one file so you can see the real diagnostics:
cfg_select! {
    windows =&gt; {
        fn platform() -&gt; &'static str { "windows" }
    }
}

enum Cmd {
    Set(String),
    Del(String),
}

fn describe(cmd: &Cmd) -&gt; String {
    match cmd {
        Cmd::Set(v) if let Ok(port) = v.parse::&lt;u16&gt;() =&gt; format!("port {port}"),
        Cmd::Del(k) =&gt; format!("delete {k}"),
    }
}

fn main() {
    println!("{} {}", platform(), describe(&Cmd::Del("x".into())));
}
error: none of the predicates in this `cfg_select` evaluated to true
 --&gt; src/main.rs:1:1
  |
1 | / cfg_select! {
2 | |     windows =&gt; {
3 | |         fn platform() -&gt; &'static str { "windows" }
4 | |     }
5 | | }
  | |_^

error[E0425]: cannot find function `platform` in this scope
  --&gt; src/main.rs:20:23
   |
20 |     println!("{} {}", platform(), describe(&Cmd::Del("x".into())));
   |                       ^^^^^^^^ not found in this scope

error[E0004]: non-exhaustive patterns: `&Cmd::Set(_)` not covered
  --&gt; src/main.rs:13:11
   |
13 |     match cmd {
   |           ^^^ pattern `&Cmd::Set(_)` not covered
   |
note: `Cmd` defined here
  --&gt; src/main.rs:7:6
   |
 7 | enum Cmd {
   |      ^^^
 8 |     Set(String),
   |     --- not covered
   = note: the matched value is of type `&Cmd`
   = note: match arms with guards don't count towards exhaustivity
help: ensure that all possible cases are being handled by adding a match arm with a wildcard pattern or an explicit pattern as shown
   |
15 ~         Cmd::Del(k) =&gt; format!("delete {k}"),
16 ~         &Cmd::Set(_) =&gt; todo!(),
   |

No true arm. This is deliberate. cfg_select! won’t silently expand to nothing, so a missing platform is a compile error on that platform rather than a confusing “cannot find function” later. (You get that second error too, as a knock-on.) Add a _ =&gt; arm whenever a fallback makes sense, and use _ =&gt; { compile_error!("…") } when it doesn’t, so the message is yours.
Guards don’t count. Even though v.parse::&lt;u16&gt;() is either Ok or Err, the compiler doesn’t reason about guards. The Rust 1.95 release notes state this explicitly. Always finish a run of guarded arms with an unguarded arm for the same pattern (or _). Don’t take the help text’s todo!() literally.

Real-World Data Modeling with Enums
Enums eliminate invalid program states at compile time:
#[derive(Debug)]
enum PaymentMethod {
    CreditCard { number: String, exp: String },
    WireTransfer { iban: String },
    InvoiceNet30 { po_number: u64 },
}

#[derive(Debug)]
struct Order {
    order_id: u64,
    amount_cents: u64,
    payment: PaymentMethod,
}

fn main() {
    let order = Order {
        order_id: 5001,
        amount_cents: 29900,
        payment: PaymentMethod::WireTransfer { iban: String::from("DE89370400440532013000") },
    };
    println!("Recorded order: {:?}", order);
}

Modules and the Filesystem
Rust’s module system organizes code into distinct namespaces and controls privacy.

The Modern Module Mapping
In modern Rust (2018/2021/2024 editions), declaring mod network; in src/main.rs resolves to src/network.rs or src/network/mod.rs.
// In src/network.rs
pub fn connect() {
    println!("Connecting to network host...");
}

The use Statement and Path Resolution
The use keyword brings paths into scope to avoid verbose qualification:
mod storage {
    pub mod cache {
        pub fn clear() {
            println!("Cache cleared.");
        }
    }
}

// Bring clear into scope
use storage::cache::clear;

fn main() {
    clear();
}

Public vs Private Visibility Rules
In Rust, everything is private by default. To expose items, use pub: - pub: Public to everyone. - pub(crate): Visible within the current crate only. - pub(super): Visible to the parent module.
mod orders {
    pub struct Order {
        pub id: u64,
        secret_notes: String, // Private field!
    }

    impl Order {
        pub fn new(id: u64, notes: String) -&gt; Self {
            Self { id, secret_notes: notes }
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let o = orders::Order::new(1, String::from("VIP customer"));
    println!("Order ID: {}", o.id);
    // println!("{}", o.secret_notes); // ERROR: private field
}

Re-exporting and Organizing Large Projects
Re-exporting using pub use decouples internal module hierarchies from your public API:
mod internal {
    pub struct Engine;
    impl Engine {
        pub fn start(&self) { println!("Engine running"); }
    }
}

// Public API
pub use internal::Engine;

fn main() {
    let e = Engine;
    e.start();
}

Vec and Dynamic Arrays
Vec&lt;T&gt; is a growable, heap-allocated array.
fn main() {
    let mut v = vec![10, 20, 30];
    v.push(40);

    for (idx, elem) in v.iter().enumerate() {
        println!("v[{}] = {}", idx, elem);
    }

    // Safe retrieval via get()
    match v.get(2) {
        Some(&val) =&gt; println!("Element at index 2 is {}", val),
        None =&gt; println!("Index out of bounds"),
    }
}

String vs &str Deep Dive
Rust strings are always valid UTF-8 sequences.
fn main() {
    let mut s = String::from("desk");
    s.push_str(" management");

    let slice: &str = &s[..4];
    println!("Owned: '{}', Slice: '{}'", s, slice);

    // Iterating bytes vs characters
    for ch in "🦀 Rust".chars() {
        println!("Char: {}", ch);
    }
}

HashMap and BTreeMap for Key-Value Storage
use std::collections::{HashMap, BTreeMap};

fn main() {
    // HashMap (O(1) average lookup)
    let mut map = HashMap::new();
    map.insert("order_1", 450);
    map.insert("order_2", 890);

    println!("Order 1 total: {:?}", map.get("order_1"));

    // BTreeMap (sorted keys, O(log N))
    let mut btree = BTreeMap::new();
    btree.insert(200, "Two Hundred");
    btree.insert(100, "One Hundred");

    for (k, v) in &btree {
        println!("Key {} -&gt; {}", k, v);
    }
}

Code Walkthrough: Demonstrating the Vulnerability
The program below shows an unseeded hasher going quadratic on chosen keys, while RandomState (SipHash) and foldhash stay linear on the same keys. Its only dependency is foldhash, added with cargo add foldhash. It was tested with rustc 1.99.1, edition 2024.

Project Setup

[package]
name = "hashdos"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
foldhash = "0.2.0"
The code resides at src/main.rs:

//! hashdos: what HashMap's default hasher protects you from.
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::{BuildHasher, BuildHasherDefault, Hasher, RandomState};
use std::time::{Duration, Instant};

/// A deliberately terrible hasher: the hash of a u64 is the u64 itself.
/// Fast, deterministic, and trivially attackable.
#[derive(Default)]
struct IdentityHasher(u64);

impl Hasher for IdentityHasher {
    fn finish(&self) -&gt; u64 {
        self.0
    }
    fn write(&mut self, bytes: &[u8]) {
        for &b in bytes {
            self.0 = (self.0 &lt;&lt; 8) | u64::from(b);
        }
    }
    fn write_u64(&mut self, n: u64) {
        self.0 = n;
    }
}

type Identity = BuildHasherDefault&lt;IdentityHasher&gt;;

fn time_inserts&lt;S: BuildHasher&gt;(build: S, keys: &[u64]) -&gt; Duration {
    let mut m: HashMap&lt;u64, u64, S&gt; = HashMap::with_hasher(build);
    let t = Instant::now();
    for &k in keys {
        m.insert(k, k);
    }
    let d = t.elapsed();
    assert_eq!(m.len(), keys.len());
    d
}

fn main() {
    // 1. The default hasher is keyed per HashMap with random keys.
    let a = RandomState::new();
    let b = RandomState::new();
    println!("same key, two RandomStates: {:016x} vs {:016x}", a.hash_one(42u64), b.hash_one(42u64));

    // 2. Iteration order therefore differs between runs of the program.
    let m: HashMap&lt;&str, i32&gt; = [("apple", 1), ("banana", 2), ("cherry", 3), ("date", 4)].into();
    println!("iteration order this run: {:?}", m.keys().collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;());

    // 3. An attacker who knows the hash function picks colliding keys.
    // With the identity hash, multiples of 2^32 share the low bits the table
    // uses to pick a bucket and the top 7 bits it uses as a tag (all zero
    // for small multipliers), so every key collides with every other.
    for n in [5_000u64, 10_000, 20_000] {
        let evil: Vec&lt;u64&gt; = (0..n).map(|i| i &lt;&lt; 32).collect();
        let benign: Vec&lt;u64&gt; = (0..n).collect();
        println!(
            "n={n:&gt;6}  identity/benign {:&gt;9.2?}  identity/evil {:&gt;10.2?}  siphash/evil {:&gt;9.2?}  foldhash/evil {:&gt;9.2?}",
            time_inserts(Identity::default(), &benign),
            time_inserts(Identity::default(), &evil),
            time_inserts(RandomState::new(), &evil),
            time_inserts(foldhash::fast::RandomState::default(), &evil),
        );
    }
}


Key Components

IdentityHasher: Hasher needs only finish and write(&[u8]). Methods like write_u64 have default implementations that call write, so overriding write_u64 lets a u64 key become its own hash with no byte shifting.
BuildHasherDefault&lt;IdentityHasher&gt;: Turns a Hasher that implements Default into a BuildHasher that makes fresh, unseeded IdentityHashers. time_inserts is generic over the BuildHasher and builds its map with HashMap::with_hasher, because the hasher is the map’s third type parameter: HashMap&lt;K, V, S&gt;.
BuildHasher::hash_one: A standard library convenience method (stable since Rust 1.71) that computes the hash of a single value without manually constructing a hasher.
Attacker Key Construction (i &lt;&lt; 32): Because IdentityHasher returns the raw u64, keys of the form \(i \times 2^{32}\) have 32 trailing zero bits (filling \(h_1\) with zero) and zero top bits for small \(i\) (filling \(h_2\) with zero). So every key starts at the same group with the same tag.

Non-Deterministic Iteration Order
Because RandomState generates new seeds at runtime, HashMap iteration order is non-deterministic. Never write tests or application code that depend on key ordering.
// BAD: Flaky test! Will fail randomly across test runs or Rust versions.
#[test]
fn test_key_order() {
    let mut map = std::collections::HashMap::new();
    map.insert("a", 1);
    map.insert("b", 2);
    assert_eq!(map.keys().copied().collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;(), vec!["a", "b"]);
}
If deterministic iteration or ordering is required: - std::collections::BTreeMap: Keys are kept sorted in \(O(\log n)\) time. - indexmap::IndexMap (from the indexmap crate): keeps insertion order, with hash-table lookups. - Explicit Sorting: Collect keys into a Vec and invoke .sort() prior to comparison.

Pitfalls and Common Mistakes

Global Hasher Overrides in Web Services
A crate-wide type HashMap = FxHashMap meant for one hot internal table ends up on maps that hold request data too. Then any client can flood them. Use the fast hasher on the specific maps that need it, by name.


Implementing Hasher by Ignoring Bytes
A Hasher that ignores some of its input is still correct: equal keys hash equally, so the map gives right answers. But every key collides, so ordinary data hits the quadratic path, no attacker needed:
// DANGEROUS: Ignores write data and returns constant
struct BrokenHasher;
impl Hasher for BrokenHasher {
    fn finish(&self) -&gt; u64 { 42 }
    fn write(&mut self, _bytes: &[u8]) {}
}


Assuming Default Hasher Stability
The Rust standard library explicitly does not guarantee that SipHash-1-3 will remain the default hasher in future releases. Never rely on specific hash outputs matching across different toolchain versions.


Floating-Point Keys
f32 and f64 implement neither Eq nor Hash. NaN != NaN breaks Eq, and +0.0 == -0.0 although their bits differ (0x00000000 and 0x80000000 for f32), so hashing the bits would break the rule that equal values hash equally. If you must key on floats, use a wrapper such as ordered-float’s OrderedFloat that defines both, or key on to_bits() after deciding how to treat -0.0 and NaN.

Code Walkthrough: Concurrent and Unsync Operations
The following runnable lab demonstrates both concurrent mutation via bbtree::BBTreeMap and single-threaded usage via bbtree::unsync::BBTreeMap.

Project Configuration
Create a Cargo project with edition 2024 and include bbtree 0.2.0 as a dependency.

[package]
name = "bbtree_lab"
version = "0.1.0"
edition = "2024"

[dependencies]
bbtree = "0.2.0"


Main Application Code
The application code in src/main.rs contains two demonstrations:

concurrent_demo(): Spawns scoped threads that concurrently insert elements into a shared BBTreeMap instance (&map), then reads values using get_copied, read, and get_many_copied.
unsync_demo(): Demonstrates single-threaded map insertion, element retrieval, and key iteration using bbtree::unsync::BBTreeMap.


use bbtree::BBTreeMap;
use bbtree::unsync::BBTreeMap as UnsyncMap;

fn concurrent_demo() {
    let map = BBTreeMap::new();
    std::thread::scope(|scope| {
        scope.spawn(|| {
            for i in 0_u64..100 {
                let _ = map.insert(i, i * 10);
            }
        });
        scope.spawn(|| {
            for i in 100_u64..200 {
                let _ = map.insert(i, i * 10);
            }
        });
    });
    println!("get_copied(42)={:?}", map.get_copied(&42));
    println!("read(150)={:?}", map.read(&150, |_, v| *v));
    let many = map.get_many_copied([&1, &42, &199, &999]);
    println!("get_many_copied={many:?}");
}

fn unsync_demo() {
    let mut map = UnsyncMap::new();
    map.insert(1, "one");
    map.insert(2, "two");
    map.insert(3, "three");
    println!("unsync get(2)={:?}", map.get(&2));
    println!("unsync keys={:?}", map.keys().copied().collect::&lt;Vec&lt;_&gt;&gt;());
}

fn main() {
    concurrent_demo();
    unsync_demo();
}
Run the application using Cargo:
cargo run --release
The execution produces output showing successful concurrent insertion, read closure execution, batch element copying, and single-threaded lookup:
