by_borrow accepts both cfg and &cfg. The blanket impls impl&lt;T&gt; Borrow&lt;T&gt; for T and impl&lt;T&gt; Borrow&lt;T&gt; for &T cover both. by_asref(&cfg) fails: &T: AsRef&lt;U&gt; only forwards when T: AsRef&lt;U&gt;, and nothing makes Config: AsRef&lt;Config&gt;. The AsRef docs explain that a reflexive blanket impl “is currently not provided due to technical restrictions of Rust’s type system (it would be overlapping with another existing blanket implementation for &T…)”. If you want generic code that accepts “a T or a &T”, write C: Borrow&lt;T&gt;. Use AsRef for “anything that can lend me a &T”.


Case 6: ToOwned is Borrow run backwards, and Cow stands on both
// src/bin/cow.rs — ToOwned is Borrow run backwards, and Cow is built on both
use std::borrow::Cow;

/// Normalise a header value: trim it, and collapse internal runs of spaces.
/// Allocates only when the input actually needs changing.
fn normalize(v: &str) -&gt; Cow&lt;'_, str&gt; {
    let t = v.trim();
    if !t.contains("  ") {
        return Cow::Borrowed(t); // zero-copy: a slice of the caller's string
    }
    let mut out = String::with_capacity(t.len());
    for word in t.split(' ').filter(|w| !w.is_empty()) {
        if !out.is_empty() {
            out.push(' ');
        }
        out.push_str(word);
    }
    Cow::Owned(out)
}

fn main() {
    for input in ["text/html", "  text/html ", "text/html;  charset=utf-8"] {
        let n = normalize(input);
        let kind = match &n {
            Cow::Borrowed(_) =&gt; "borrowed",
            Cow::Owned(_) =&gt; "owned (allocated)",
        };
        println!("{input:&lt;28} -&gt; {n:&lt;26} {kind}");
    }

    // &lt;str as ToOwned&gt;::Owned = String, and String: Borrow&lt;str&gt;: the pair Cow&lt;str&gt; relies on.
    let owned: String = "abc".to_owned();
    let back: &str = std::borrow::Borrow::borrow(&owned);
    println!("to_owned -&gt; {owned:?}, borrow -&gt; {back:?}");

    // into_owned() allocates only if it still has to.
    let c: Cow&lt;'_, str&gt; = normalize("plain");
    let s: String = c.into_owned();
    println!("into_owned -&gt; {s:?}");
}
$ cargo run -q --bin cow
text/html                    -&gt; text/html                  borrowed
  text/html                  -&gt; text/html                  borrowed
text/html;  charset=utf-8    -&gt; text/html; charset=utf-8   owned (allocated)
to_owned -&gt; "abc", borrow -&gt; "abc"
into_owned -&gt; "plain"
Cow&lt;'a, B&gt; requires B: ToOwned, and ToOwned requires Self::Owned: Borrow&lt;Self&gt;. That pair lets a Cow&lt;str&gt; hold either a &str or a String and deref to &str either way. normalize allocates only for the input that needs rewriting. The other two return a slice of the caller’s string.

The trap
The trap is treating Borrow as “AsRef with a better name for maps”, and implementing it whenever you want get(&str) to compile. Case 3 compiles and passes a lowercase test, and in production it returns None for "Content-Type". The same class of bug appears whenever Borrow&lt;T&gt; lends a form whose Hash, Eq or Ord differ from the owner’s:

a struct that borrows as one of its fields (User: Borrow&lt;u64&gt; returning &self.id, while User’s Eq compares every field);
a float wrapper with a total order that borrows as f64 (whose PartialOrd disagrees for NaN);
a path-like key that normalises on Eq but borrows as the raw string.

The related Deref trap is newtype “inheritance”: struct Email(String) with Deref&lt;Target = String&gt; so that email.len() works. Every String method becomes part of the Email API, email.to_uppercase() silently returns a plain String, and the std docs’ conditions (“transparently behaves like a value of the target type”, “will not be surprised by any deref coercion behavior”) no longer hold. Implement AsRef&lt;str&gt; and a few explicit methods instead.

The type-state connection
The states are empty structs that are never constructed. They exist only as type arguments.
use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::mem::size_of;

// States are zero-sized marker types. They never exist at runtime.
pub struct Idle;
pub struct Connected;
pub struct Authed;

pub struct Conn&lt;S&gt; {
    host: String,
    log: Vec&lt;String&gt;,
    _state: PhantomData&lt;S&gt;,
}

impl Conn&lt;Idle&gt; {
    pub fn new(host: &str) -&gt; Self {
        Conn { host: host.to_string(), log: vec![], _state: PhantomData }
    }
    pub fn connect(mut self) -&gt; Conn&lt;Connected&gt; {
        self.log.push(format!("connect {}", self.host));
        Conn { host: self.host, log: self.log, _state: PhantomData }
    }
}

impl Conn&lt;Connected&gt; {
    pub fn login(mut self, user: &str) -&gt; Result&lt;Conn&lt;Authed&gt;, Conn&lt;Connected&gt;&gt; {
        if user.is_empty() {
            return Err(self); // stay in Connected, caller keeps the value
        }
        self.log.push(format!("login {user}"));
        Ok(Conn { host: self.host, log: self.log, _state: PhantomData })
    }
}

impl Conn&lt;Authed&gt; {
    pub fn query(&mut self, q: &str) -&gt; usize {
        self.log.push(format!("query {q}"));
        q.len()
    }
    pub fn close(self) -&gt; Vec&lt;String&gt; {
        self.log
    }
}

// Methods every state shares go on the generic impl.
impl&lt;S&gt; Conn&lt;S&gt; {
    pub fn host(&self) -&gt; &str { &self.host }
}
Three design choices carry the pattern:

Transitions take self by value. connect(self) -&gt; Conn&lt;Connected&gt; consumes the idle connection, so the old state can’t be used again.
Fallible transitions return the old state on failure. login returns Result&lt;Conn&lt;Authed&gt;, Conn&lt;Connected&gt;&gt;. A failed login hands back the still-connected value instead of dropping it.
Shared behaviour goes on impl&lt;S&gt; Conn&lt;S&gt;. host() works in every state, so it isn’t copied three times.

The driver:
fn main() {
    let c = Conn::new("db.internal");
    println!("size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Idle&gt;&gt;()   = {}", size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Idle&gt;&gt;());
    println!("size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;() = {}", size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;());
    println!("size_of::&lt;PhantomData&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;() = {}", size_of::&lt;PhantomData&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;());
    let c = c.connect();
    let c = match c.login("") {
        Ok(_) =&gt; unreachable!(),
        Err(still_connected) =&gt; { println!("empty user rejected, still on {}", still_connected.host()); still_connected }
    };
    let mut c = c.login("kanthi").ok().expect("login");
    let n = c.query("SELECT 1");
    println!("query returned {n}");
    for line in c.close() { println!("log: {line}"); }
}
size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Idle&gt;&gt;()   = 48
size_of::&lt;Conn&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;() = 48
size_of::&lt;PhantomData&lt;Authed&gt;&gt;() = 0
empty user rejected, still on db.internal
query returned 8
log: connect db.internal
log: login kanthi
log: query SELECT 1
Conn&lt;Idle&gt; and Conn&lt;Authed&gt; are both 48 bytes (a String and a Vec, 24 bytes each on x86-64), and PhantomData&lt;Authed&gt; is 0. The state costs nothing at runtime: there’s no tag byte and no branch. Each impl block is monomorphised separately, so query only exists for Conn&lt;Authed&gt;.

1. The swapped-argument bug
// Two IDs with the same representation: the compiler cannot tell them apart.
fn transfer(from_account: u64, to_account: u64, cents: u64) {
    println!("move {cents} cents from {from_account} to {to_account}");
}

#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
struct AccountId(u64);
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
struct Cents(u64);

fn transfer_typed(from: AccountId, to: AccountId, amount: Cents) {
    println!("move {} cents from {} to {}", amount.0, from.0, to.0);
}

fn main() {
    let (alice, bob) = (1001_u64, 2002_u64);
    transfer(alice, 500, bob); // compiles; bob's id is now the amount

    let (alice, bob) = (AccountId(1001), AccountId(2002));
    transfer_typed(alice, bob, Cents(500));
}
$ cargo run -q --bin swapped
move 2002 cents from 1001 to 500
move 500 cents from 1001 to 2002
The first call moves 2002 cents to account 500. Every argument is a u64, so nothing could catch the mistake. Here is the same mistake with newtypes:
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
struct AccountId(u64);
#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug)]
struct Cents(u64);

fn transfer_typed(from: AccountId, to: AccountId, amount: Cents) {
    println!("{from:?} {to:?} {amount:?}");
}

fn main() {
    let (alice, bob) = (AccountId(1001), AccountId(2002));
    transfer_typed(alice, Cents(500), bob);
}
$ cargo build -q --bin swapped_typed_bug
error[E0308]: arguments to this function are incorrect
  --&gt; src/bin/swapped_typed_bug.rs:12:5
   |
12 |     transfer_typed(alice, Cents(500), bob);
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^        ----------  --- expected `Cents`, found `AccountId`
   |                           |
   |                           expected `AccountId`, found `Cents`
   |
note: function defined here
  --&gt; src/bin/swapped_typed_bug.rs:6:4
   |
 6 | fn transfer_typed(from: AccountId, to: AccountId, amount: Cents) {
   |    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: swap these arguments
   |
12 -     transfer_typed(alice, Cents(500), bob);
12 +     transfer_typed(alice, bob, Cents(500));
   |
The compiler rejects the call and even suggests the fix. Deriving Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash keeps IDs as convenient as plain integers in hash maps and comparisons. Leave out Add and the other arithmetic traits unless the operation makes sense: adding two account IDs never does. The Rust API Guidelines list this as C-NEWTYPE: “Newtypes provide static distinctions”.

2. Zero cost, and what repr(transparent) adds
use std::mem::{align_of, size_of};

#[allow(dead_code)]
struct Meters(f64);
#[allow(dead_code)]
#[repr(transparent)]
struct UserId(u32);
#[allow(dead_code)]
struct RowId(std::num::NonZeroU32);

fn main() {
    println!("f64: size {} align {}", size_of::&lt;f64&gt;(), align_of::&lt;f64&gt;());
    println!("Meters: size {} align {}", size_of::&lt;Meters&gt;(), align_of::&lt;Meters&gt;());
    println!("Option&lt;u32&gt;: size {}", size_of::&lt;Option&lt;u32&gt;&gt;());
    println!("Option&lt;UserId&gt;: size {}", size_of::&lt;Option&lt;UserId&gt;&gt;());
    println!("Option&lt;std::num::NonZeroU32&gt;: size {}", size_of::&lt;Option&lt;std::num::NonZeroU32&gt;&gt;());
    println!("Option&lt;RowId&gt;: size {}", size_of::&lt;Option&lt;RowId&gt;&gt;());
    println!("[UserId; 1000]: size {}", size_of::&lt;[UserId; 1000]&gt;());
}
$ cargo run -q --bin zero_cost
f64: size 8 align 8
Meters: size 8 align 8
Option&lt;u32&gt;: size 8
Option&lt;UserId&gt;: size 8
Option&lt;std::num::NonZeroU32&gt;: size 4
Option&lt;RowId&gt;: size 4
[UserId; 1000]: size 4000

Meters has the same size and alignment as f64, and an array of 1000 UserIds is exactly 4000 bytes. There is no header and no tag.
Niches survive the wrapper. Option&lt;RowId&gt; is 4 bytes because RowId wraps NonZeroU32, and the compiler reuses the forbidden zero as None. Option&lt;UserId&gt; over a plain u32 has no niche, so it needs the extra discriminant and is 8 bytes.

What you measure for a default-representation (repr(Rust)) struct is not a promise. The Reference’s type-layout chapter gives repr(Rust) very few guarantees. #[repr(transparent)] is the attribute that guarantees “the same layout and ABI as the only non-size 0 non-alignment 1 field”. You need it whenever unsafe code or FFI depends on the layout:
// #[repr(transparent)] guarantees UserId has the same layout and ABI as u32,
// so a &[u32] from storage or FFI can be viewed as &[UserId] without copying.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
#[repr(transparent)]
struct UserId(u32);

fn as_user_ids(raw: &[u32]) -&gt; &[UserId] {
    // SAFETY: UserId is repr(transparent) over u32, so size, alignment and
    // validity are identical; the lifetime is carried over from `raw`.
    unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(raw.as_ptr().cast::&lt;UserId&gt;(), raw.len()) }
}

fn main() {
    let raw = vec![7_u32, 42, 99];
    let ids = as_user_ids(&raw);
    println!("{ids:?}");
    println!("same address: {}", raw.as_ptr() as usize == ids.as_ptr() as usize);
}
$ cargo run -q --bin transparent
[UserId(7), UserId(42), UserId(99)]
same address: true
Without #[repr(transparent)], this cast would be relying on layout the language doesn’t promise. With it, a &[u32] from a file, a database page or a C library becomes a typed &[UserId] with no copy (same address: true). The Nomicon describes the same rule for passing newtypes across extern "C" boundaries.

3. Validated values: parse, don’t re-check
// A newtype whose only constructor validates: holding an Email *proves* it was checked.
mod email {
    #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
    pub struct Email(String); // private field: no Email("junk".into()) outside this module

    #[derive(Debug)]
    pub struct InvalidEmail(pub String);

    impl TryFrom&lt;String&gt; for Email {
        type Error = InvalidEmail;
        fn try_from(s: String) -&gt; Result&lt;Self, Self::Error&gt; {
            match s.split_once('@') {
                Some((user, host)) if !user.is_empty() && host.contains('.') =&gt; {
                    Ok(Email(s.to_ascii_lowercase()))
                }
                _ =&gt; Err(InvalidEmail(s)),
            }
        }
    }

    impl AsRef&lt;str&gt; for Email {
        fn as_ref(&self) -&gt; &str {
            &self.0
        }
    }
}

use email::Email;

fn send_welcome(to: &Email) {
    println!("sending to {}", to.as_ref());
}

fn main() {
    for raw in ["Ada@Example.org", "no-at-sign", "@example.org", "bob@localhost"] {
        match Email::try_from(raw.to_string()) {
            Ok(e) =&gt; send_welcome(&e),
            Err(err) =&gt; println!("rejected: {:?}", err.0),
        }
    }
}
$ cargo run -q --bin validated
sending to ada@example.org
rejected: "no-at-sign"
rejected: "@example.org"
rejected: "bob@localhost"
The field is private, so outside mod email the only way to get an Email is TryFrom, which validates it and normalises it to lower case. send_welcome(&Email) has no reason to check again. Trying to bypass the constructor fails:
mod email {
    #[derive(Debug)]
    pub struct Email(String);
}

fn main() {
    let e = email::Email("not an email".to_string());
    println!("{e:?}");
}
$ cargo build -q --bin validated_bug
error[E0603]: tuple struct constructor `Email` is private
 --&gt; src/bin/validated_bug.rs:7:20
  |
3 |     pub struct Email(String);
  |                      ------ a constructor is private if any of the fields is private
...
7 |     let e = email::Email("not an email".to_string());
  |                    ^^^^^ private tuple struct constructor
  |
note: the tuple struct constructor `Email` is defined here
 --&gt; src/bin/validated_bug.rs:3:5
  |
3 |     pub struct Email(String);
  |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
help: consider making the field publicly accessible
  |
3 |     pub struct Email(pub String);
  |                      +++
The compiler’s help suggests pub String. In this case that is the wrong fix, because it would let anyone build an unvalidated Email. The privacy is the whole point.
The validation here is deliberately small. Real email validation belongs in a dedicated crate. The pattern stays the same whatever rule you plug in.

4. Getting around the orphan rule
You can’t implement a trait from another crate (Display) for a type from another crate (Vec&lt;String&gt;):
use std::fmt;

impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter&lt;'_&gt;) -&gt; fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.join(","))
    }
}

fn main() {}
$ cargo build -q --bin orphan_bug
error[E0117]: only traits defined in the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate
 --&gt; src/bin/orphan_bug.rs:3:1
  |
3 | impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; {
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^-----------
  |                       |
  |                       `Vec` is not defined in the current crate
  |
  = note: impl doesn't have any local type before any uncovered type parameters
  = note: for more information see https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/items/implementations.html#orphan-rules
  = note: define and implement a trait or new type instead
The last note gives the fix, “define and implement a trait or new type instead”:
use std::fmt;

// impl fmt::Display for Vec&lt;String&gt; { ... }  // E0117: only traits defined in
// the current crate can be implemented for types defined outside of the crate

struct Csv(Vec&lt;String&gt;);

impl fmt::Display for Csv {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter&lt;'_&gt;) -&gt; fmt::Result {
        write!(f, "{}", self.0.join(","))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let row = Csv(vec!["id".into(), "name".into(), "email".into()]);
    println!("{row}");
}
$ cargo run -q --bin orphan
id,name,email
The Book presents exactly this use. The orphan rules exist so that two crates can never provide conflicting impls of the same trait for the same type.

Annotating Lifetimes in Functions and Structs
When a function returns a reference that could come from multiple inputs, explicit lifetime annotations are required:
// The returned reference is guaranteed valid for the intersection of 'a
fn longest&lt;'a&gt;(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -&gt; &'a str {
    if x.len() &gt; y.len() { x } else { y }
}

struct Excerpt&lt;'a&gt; {
    part: &'a str,
}

fn main() {
    let string1 = String::from("desk configuration");
    let result;
    {
        let string2 = String::from("orders");
        result = longest(string1.as_str(), string2.as_str());
        println!("Longest string: {}", result);
    }
}

Lifetime Bounds on Traits
Constrain types with lifetime bounds, such as T: 'a (meaning T must outlive lifetime 'a):
use std::fmt::Display;

fn print_ref&lt;'a, T&gt;(item: &'a T)
where
    T: Display + 'a,
{
    println!("Item: {}", item);
}

Covariant: shared references shrink freely
// &'a T is covariant in 'a: a longer-lived reference can stand in for a shorter one.
fn pick&lt;'a&gt;(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -&gt; &'a str { if x.len() &gt;= y.len() { x } else { y } }

fn main() {
    let forever: &'static str = "static text";
    let owned = String::from("local");
    let r = pick(forever, &owned); // &'static str shrinks to the local lifetime
    println!("{r}");
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 cov.rs && ./cov
static text
pick needs a single 'a for both arguments. The compiler chooses 'a as the lifetime of owned and shrinks the &'static str to fit. That is safe because a shared reference can only be read, and reading something for less time than it lives is never a problem.

Invariant: &mut T can’t shrink its T
Now make the shrinking happen behind a &mut:
// &'a mut T is invariant in T: this is what keeps the next line from compiling.
fn overwrite&lt;T&gt;(slot: &mut T, value: T) { *slot = value; }

fn main() {
    let mut s: &'static str = "static text";
    {
        let local = String::from("short-lived");
        overwrite(&mut s, &local);
    }
    println!("{s}");
}
$ rustc --edition 2024 inv_mut.rs && ./inv_mut
