Implementing the trait is a safety contract
unsafe impl Allocator is a promise the compiler cannot check. The ways to break it are the classic C allocator bugs: handing out overlapping blocks (a bump cursor that forgets alignment padding), returning memory that dies before the container does (an allocator that owns its buffer by value and gets moved), or panicking inside allocate. The stabilized contract explicitly forbids unwinding out of any Allocator method, from its clones, or from its drop. Keep the unsafe impl small, put the bookkeeping in plain safe code, and test it under Miri (cargo +nightly miri test), which catches the use-after-free, out-of-bounds, and aliasing violations that a broken allocator causes in the containers using it.

The problem new_cyclic solves
Sometimes a value needs a non-owning handle to its own Arc allocation — a classic example is a node that stores me: Weak&lt;Node&gt; for callbacks, or a child that should hold parent: Weak&lt;Node&gt; while the parent is still being published as an Arc.
The awkward pattern without new_cyclic looks like:
// Awkward: temporary None, then fix up — often requires interior mutability.
struct Node {
    me: std::sync::Mutex&lt;Weak&lt;Node&gt;&gt;,
}
Or splitting construction across OnceLock fields. Arc::new_cyclic gives you a Weak&lt;T&gt; while the T is being built, then finishes the Arc in one step.
pub fn new_cyclic&lt;F&gt;(data_fn: F) -&gt; Arc&lt;T&gt;
where
    F: FnOnce(&Weak&lt;T&gt;) -&gt; T;
Rc::new_cyclic is the single-threaded twin in std::rc.

Lab: self weak and parent weak

use std::sync::{Arc, Weak};

#[derive(Debug)]
struct Node {
    name: &'static str,
    me: Weak&lt;Node&gt;,
    parent: Weak&lt;Node&gt;,
}

fn main() {
    let root: Arc&lt;Node&gt; = Arc::new_cyclic(|w| Node {
        name: "root",
        me: w.clone(),
        parent: Weak::new(),
    });
    println!(
        "root={} strong={} weak={} me_upgrades={}",
        root.name,
        Arc::strong_count(&root),
        Arc::weak_count(&root),
        root.me.upgrade().is_some()
    );

    let child: Arc&lt;Node&gt; = Arc::new_cyclic(|w| Node {
        name: "child",
        me: w.clone(),
        parent: Arc::downgrade(&root),
    });
    println!(
        "child={} parent={:?} strong={} weak={}",
        child.name,
        child.parent.upgrade().map(|p| p.name),
        Arc::strong_count(&child),
        Arc::weak_count(&child)
    );

    let weak_root = Arc::downgrade(&root);
    drop(root);
    println!(
        "after drop root: weak_root alive={}",
        weak_root.upgrade().is_some()
    );
    println!(
        "child parent still? {:?}",
        child.parent.upgrade().map(|p| p.name)
    );
}
