Reading the counters:

After construction, root has one strong owner and one weak (me).
me.upgrade() succeeds outside the closure.
child.parent is a weak link to root. Dropping the last strong root frees it; the child’s weak parent no longer upgrades. There is no strong cycle, so memory is reclaimed.

Single-threaded twin: Rc::new_cyclic
use std::rc::{Rc, Weak};

struct Tick {
    me: Weak&lt;Tick&gt;,
    n: u32,
}

fn main() {
    let t = Rc::new_cyclic(|w| Tick { me: w.clone(), n: 1 });
    assert!(t.me.upgrade().is_some());
    assert_eq!(Rc::strong_count(&t), 1);
    println!("n={}", t.n);
}
Prefer Rc when the graph never crosses threads — atomic refcounts in Arc are unnecessary cost. The closure rules are identical: no successful upgrade during construction.

The Iterator Trait and Protocol
In Rust, iterators are lazy sequences that produce values on demand via the Iterator trait:
fn main() {
    let v = vec![1, 2, 3];
    let mut iter = v.iter();

    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&1));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&2));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), Some(&3));
    assert_eq!(iter.next(), None);
}

Creating Custom Iterators
struct Counter {
    count: usize,
}

impl Counter {
    fn new() -&gt; Self { Self { count: 0 } }
}

impl Iterator for Counter {
    type Item = usize;
    fn next(&mut self) -&gt; Option&lt;Self::Item&gt; {
        if self.count &lt; 3 {
            self.count += 1;
            Some(self.count)
        } else {
            None
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let counter = Counter::new();
    let items: Vec&lt;usize&gt; = counter.collect();
    println!("Custom iterator output: {:?}", items);
}

Closures and Capture Semantics
Closures are anonymous functions that can capture environment variables: - Fn: Borrows values from the environment immutably. - FnMut: Borrows values mutably. - FnOnce: Takes ownership and consumes captured values.
fn main() {
    let factor = 3;
    let multiply = |x| x * factor;
    println!("Result: {}", multiply(10));
}

Higher-Order Functions and Combinators
fn main() {
    let numbers = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

    let sum_of_even_squares: i32 = numbers
        .iter()
        .filter(|&&x| x % 2 == 0)
        .map(|&x| x * x)
        .sum();

